WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.420
Hi everyone, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 46 from May 22nd, 2026.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.040
Today's AI news is pretty exciting, and I promise no jargon, let's go.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:18.320
So imagine you open an app and instead of scrolling through endless shows, it just knows exactly

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:24.340
what you want to hear. That is basically what Spotify just launched with a new standalone app called

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:30.900
Studio by Spotify Labs. Think of it like having a personal radio producer who stayed up all night

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:36.980
studying your playlists, your calendar, and even notes you saved. You type a simple prompt in chat,

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:42.580
and the app pulls together your listening history plus connected apps like email to create a custom

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:49.480
daily briefing or even a full podcast episode just for you. The AI does not simply queue up old tracks,

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:55.100
it generates fresh spoken words that sound like a friendly host who studied your tastes overnight.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:01.160
This matters because it shows AI moving from answering one question at a time to actually

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:07.480
creating audio you can enjoy while walking to class or riding the bus. For students, it could turn

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:13.260
a dull homework topic into an engaging summary you listen to on the way home. For anyone curious

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:18.540
about AI, it highlights the shift toward tools that make new media instead of just replying.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:24.780
You can try it right now by opening the Studio app on your computer, connecting your Spotify account

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:30.680
and typing something like make a 10 minute podcast about new artists similar to the ones I played last

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:36.660
week. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, The Deep Dive, where we look under the hood,

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:42.480
let us break down how an AI agent actually works using the Spotify example as our guide.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:48.060
Think of an agent like a helpful assistant who follows a to-do list you never had to write down

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:54.280
yourself. First, it gathers raw material almost like a student collecting notes from different classes

00:01:54.280 --> 00:02:01.260
before starting an essay. Next, it decides the order and tone, the way a band plans which songs go

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:07.920
where in a set list so the energy feels right. Then it turns that plan into spoken words using text

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:14.160
to speech technology that makes everything sound natural and human. And that is basically what the AI

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:20.260
agent does when it builds your daily briefing. It chains together several small tasks to finish one

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:27.220
bigger project. Not so scary, right? Let us move into some cool stuff you can try yourself. First up,

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:33.480
an AI system tried to run a graduation ceremony at a community college in Arizona and things did not

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:39.720
go smoothly. The system mispronounced names, showed wrong names on screens, and even skipped some

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:45.400
students entirely. The president had to stop everything and switch back to a human announcer so

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:51.320
everyone could walk the stage properly. This reminds us that AI can look amazing in demos yet fall

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:57.080
apart during real moments that matter, like family events where accuracy counts. You can read

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:02.380
the full thread on Reddit in the artificial sub-reddit to see how people reacted and what lessons they

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:09.000
shared. Next, people on Reddit notice something funny happening with chat GPT. After getting one

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:15.240
good answer, users quickly jump from asking simple questions like explain Python loops to demanding the

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:22.040
AI build entire apps or launch businesses overnight. The confident tone makes those wild plans sound

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:27.820
suddenly realistic. Try this yourself by opening any free chatbot and asking it to create a simple

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:34.680
plan for a weekend hobby project. Then notice how polished it sounds. After that, double check the

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:40.800
steps with a real person or a tutorial site so you keep your feet on the ground. In QuickBits today,

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:46.920
AI smart glasses still face a basic hurdle. The dream of lightweight glasses that understand

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:52.360
everything you see runs into the fact that they need constant fast uploads to the cloud.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:57.860
Current cell networks simply are not built to handle that many live video streams at once.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:04.180
Also in QuickBits, China showed off a giant robot that can switch between running on four legs

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:10.660
and standing upright. The 10 foot tall machine weighs about 1000 pounds and was built for moving through

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:16.160
city spaces. It is an interesting look at how robots are getting better at handling different kinds

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:21.900
of movement without getting stuck. And that is a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go,

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:34.160
huh, that's cool. Go play with it. Curiosity is how every expert started. See you tomorrow. And if

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:41.220
this episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice. Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
